{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Task 2: measuring NSD, then trying to move it",
  "description": "A branch on smri-fm, MedARC's structural-MRI foundation model repo. It does two things to task 2 of the FOMO26 challenge, which asks a model to outline a meningioma in a brain MRI. First it makes the scoring code measure NSD, a boundary metric worth half the task's rank that nobody had ever computed. Then it replaces the part of the model that draws the outline, so the outline is no longer made of 8mm blocks. The second change turns out to help the metric that was already measured and not the one that was just added, and the reason is in the scans rather than in the model.",
  "kind": "prep",
  "pr": {
    "base": "11e53ab1d4bf29d3b44e3b59c7e6166233d414e1",
    "head": "c1862e09cddce1f13112d6540aa666e55ec60c6d"
  },
  "map": "flowchart TB\n  subgraph setup[\"setup\"]\n    dep[\"pyproject.toml\"]\n    cfg[\"Config\"]\n  end\n  subgraph method[\"method: the part we tune\"]\n    decoder[\"ConvDecoder\"]\n    loss[\"segmentation_loss\"]\n    tokengrid[\"token_grid\"]\n    fit[\"Task2Method.fit\"]\n    proba[\"predict_proba\"]\n  end\n  subgraph protocol[\"protocol: held fixed\"]\n    nsd[\"normalized_surface_distance\"]\n    thr[\"THRESHOLDS\"]\n    curves[\"subject_curves\"]\n    loo[\"leave_one_out\"]\n    score[\"score\"]\n  end\n  subgraph out[\"what came out\"]\n    ran[\"the runs\"]\n    dice[\"Dice result\"]\n    surf[\"NSD result\"]\n    left[\"what is still broken\"]\n    close[\"recap\"]\n  end\n  cfg --> decoder\n  dep --> nsd\n  decoder --> loss --> fit\n  tokengrid --> fit --> proba\n  proba --> curves\n  nsd --> curves\n  thr --> curves --> loo --> score --> ran\n  ran --> dice --> surf --> left --> close",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 222,
      "node": "close",
      "title": "A branch that measures half a score, then tries to move it",
      "summary": "Orientation. What this repo is, what task 2 asks for, and what the branch's three commits do.",
      "description": "`smri-fm` is a repository belonging to **MedARC**, an open medical-AI research collective. The software in it trains and evaluates a *foundation model* for structural brain MRI, meaning one large model pretrained on many scans and then reused for many different clinical questions rather than trained from scratch for each one.\n\nThis is a tour of your own branch, `seg/task2-finetune`, three commits sitting on top of the main branch and not yet in a pull request.\n\nThe branch is aimed at one job from the **FOMO26 challenge**, a public competition the group is entering. Task 2 of that challenge gives you a brain MRI and asks for a mask: for every voxel in the scan, is this tumour or not. The tumour is a *meningioma*, a growth on the membranes that wrap the brain. You get 23 patients to learn from, and the organisers score you on 107 patients you never see.\n\nTwo things prompted the branch. The challenge scores each submission on **two** numbers and ranks you on the average of your rank in each, and the repository's scoring code only ever computed one of them, so half the score was invisible. Separately, the part of the model that turns its understanding into an actual outline could only draw in 8mm blocks, which is coarse next to a tumour.\n\nIn this tour we will start with why the blocks were there, then walk the code the branch adds. Steps 3 to 10 follow the scoring path, which is where the missing metric goes. Steps 11 to 15 follow the model path, which is where the blocks go. Step 16 restates the whole route. Steps 17 to 20 are what the runs actually produced, including one result that contradicts the reason the change was made. Step 21 is a set of questions to test yourself with.\n\nThe conclusion, so it is not a surprise: the new model is a clear win on the metric that was already being measured and not on the one that was just added, and [step 19](#step-19) argues that the reason has nothing to do with the model.\n\n![The pipeline for one subject, with the head and the scoring step marked as the two things this branch changes.](.youngblood/assets/task2-finetune/fig1-system.png)\n\nThe line you are parked on sits in [`predict_proba`](#L217), the function in [`main_task2.py`](#L1) that turns a scan into a probability for every voxel. It is the single line that most defines the branch, and every part of it will make sense by [step 15](#step-15)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 93,
      "node": "decoder",
      "title": "Why the old outline was made of 8mm blocks",
      "summary": "The encoder chops the scan into 8mm cubes. The old head emitted one number per cube and copied it to every voxel inside.",
      "description": "We are in [`main_task2.py`](#L1), the file that holds task 2 from end to end, and the walk has jumped back from the line [step 1](#step-1) parked on to the top of the file. Before any code, the situation the branch is responding to.\n\nThe pretrained model is a **vision transformer**, a network architecture that cannot look at every voxel of a scan individually because there are far too many of them. So the scan is diced into cubes first. Here each cube is **8 x 8 x 8 mm**, which is 512 voxels, and the whole 208 x 240 x 208 volume becomes a grid of 26 x 30 x 26 cubes. Each cube is summarised as one vector of 1024 numbers, called a **token**.\n\nThat part is fixed. It is baked into the pretrained weights, and changing it means pretraining a new model, which the challenge rules do not allow.\n\nWhat was not fixed is what happened next. The old code put a *logistic regression* on each token, a simple statistical model that takes the 1024 numbers and returns a single probability. One number per cube. To turn that into a mask, the number was copied to all 512 voxels of its cube, and then a single cutoff was applied to the whole volume. So after cutting, a cube was either entirely tumour or entirely not, and the finest outline the model could draw was a staircase of 8mm steps.\n\n![The same reference outline as a block staircase and as a per-voxel boundary.](.youngblood/assets/task2-finetune/fig2-staircase.png)\n\nThe class you are parked on, [`ConvDecoder`](#L93-L114), is what this branch puts there instead, and it is the subject of [step 11](#step-11). Everything between here and there is the scoring work that had to come first, because without it there is no way to tell whether the replacement helped."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 1,
      "node": "cfg",
      "title": "One file, split into a part you tune and a part you must not",
      "summary": "main_task2.py is task 2 end to end. Its module docstring draws a line down the middle that the whole repo depends on.",
      "description": "Everything in this tour happens in one file, [`main_task2.py`](#L1), which holds task 2 from beginning to end: loading the 23 patients, running the model, scoring it, and writing out a submission. The repository has one of these per challenge task, deliberately duplicated rather than sharing a base class, and its `AGENTS.md` says so in as many words.\n\nThe file is cut in half, and the halves have very different rules. The docstring at the top states it:\n\n- **The method** is [`Task2Method`](#L129), the model and the head. This is the part you are supposed to change. Tuning the model means editing this half.\n- **The protocol** is everything below the divider at [line 267](#L267): how the 23 patients are split, which metrics are computed, how the confidence intervals are drawn. It is *frozen*. Changing it means every score the team has already written down stops being comparable to every score they write down afterwards.\n\nThat matters here because this branch changes both halves. The metric work is a protocol change and it needed a deliberate decision, which [step 9](#step-9) comes back to. The decoder is a method change and is exactly what the method half is for.\n\nOne more rule from that docstring, at [line 15](#L15): the scoring path and the submission path both go through the same function, so every scored fold exercises the code that will actually run inside the competition container. Nothing is special-cased for evaluation.\n\nThe walk starts on the protocol side, because the metric had to exist before the decoder could be judged."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 356,
      "node": "score",
      "title": "The function that reports the result, and what it left out",
      "summary": "score turns the raw curves into the numbers that get written down. Before this branch it reported Dice and nothing else.",
      "description": "We are in the protocol half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1), the frozen part described in [step 3](#step-3). [`score`](#L356-L381) is the last thing the protocol does: it takes the raw measurements from all 23 patients and reduces them to the handful of numbers that get written into a results file and pasted into the team's table.\n\nBefore this branch it returned five numbers and every one of them described a single metric: the mean, a low and a high end for a confidence interval, an oracle value, and the cutoff it chose.\n\n**Dice** is the standard overlap score for a mask. Take the voxels the model called tumour and the voxels that really are tumour, count how many are in both, double it, and divide by the two counts added together. Perfect agreement is 1, no overlap at all is 0. It is a measure of *volume*: did you find roughly the right amount of tumour in roughly the right place.\n\nThe challenge does not rank on Dice alone. It also computes **NSD**, the normalized surface distance, which is a measure of *boundary*: of all the points on the outline, what fraction sit within a millimetre of where the outline should be. A blob can score well on Dice and badly on NSD by covering the right volume with a wrong-shaped edge. The final ranking is the average of your Dice rank and your NSD rank, so the two count equally, and this function was blind to one of them.\n\nSo every decision anyone made about task 2, which head to use, which cutoff, whether to keep a filter, was made on half the score. Fixing that is the first commit, and it starts with a dependency."
    },
    {
      "file": "pyproject.toml",
      "line": 32,
      "node": "dep",
      "title": "The metric library comes from git because the published one is broken",
      "summary": "surface-distance is on pypi, and that build crashes on modern numpy in a case this code reaches. The dependency points at the source repo instead.",
      "description": "[`pyproject.toml`](#L1) is the file that lists every third-party package this project needs, in one flat list. This branch adds one entry to it.\n\nThe challenge organisers publish their own scoring code, and it computes NSD by calling **`surface-distance`**, a small library from Google DeepMind that does the geometry of comparing two surfaces. Matching them exactly matters: a metric computed a slightly different way is not the metric you will be ranked on.\n\nThat library is on **PyPI**, the standard Python package index, so the obvious entry is its name and nothing else. That was tried and it does not work. The published build is an old snapshot that uses `np.Inf`, a spelling of infinity that **NumPy** (the array library everything here is built on) removed in its version 2 release. This project runs NumPy 2.4.\n\nThe crash is not hypothetical. That spelling sits in the branch of the library that runs when one of the two masks is empty, and the scoring code below deliberately sweeps a cutoff so high that the model predicts nothing at all. Running it produces `AttributeError: np.Inf was removed in the NumPy 2.0 release`.\n\nDeepMind's own repository has the fix. So the dependency is written as a **direct git reference**, a form pip and `uv` both understand, pointing at the source rather than at the published build. The [comment above it](#L31) records why, because otherwise the next person tidies it back to a plain name and the crash returns.\n\n<details><summary>The two versions, side by side, on an empty prediction</summary>\n\n```\n=== PyPI wheel ===\nidentical       1.0\ndisjoint        0.0\nempty pred     RAISED AttributeError `np.Inf` was removed in the NumPy 2.0 release.\n\n=== DeepMind master ===\nidentical       1.0\ndisjoint        0.0\nempty pred      0.0\n```\n\nEverything else about the two files is identical apart from that spelling and the same change to `np.NaN`.\n</details>\n\nThe library is now installable. [Step 6](#step-6) is the code that calls it."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 286,
      "node": "nsd",
      "title": "A ten-line wrapper that has to match the organisers exactly",
      "summary": "normalized_surface_distance is the whole of the new metric. Its job is to be identical to the challenge's own wrapper, including a rule that looks like a bug.",
      "description": "Back in [`main_task2.py`](#L1), in the frozen protocol half, this is the only new function the metric commit adds. [`normalized_surface_distance`](#L286-L298) takes a predicted mask, a true mask, and the physical size of a voxel, and returns one number between 0 and 1.\n\nThe two library calls at [lines 296 and 297](#L296-L297) do all the geometry. The first builds the two surfaces and measures how far every point on each is from the other; the second asks what fraction of that surface agrees within a tolerance, here **1 millimetre**, which is the value the organisers use.\n\nThe interesting part is the [guard above them](#L294-L295), which returns 0 whenever either mask is empty. That is copied from the organisers' wrapper and it is deliberately inconsistent with their own Dice. If the model predicts nothing on a patient who has no tumour, their Dice calls that a perfect 1 and their NSD calls it a 0. Matching a rule you would not have chosen is the entire point of a metric implementation, so the asymmetry is preserved and [a test](src/fomo_tune/test_main_task2.py#L55-L60) pins it in place so nobody helpfully repairs it later.\n\nThe `import` sits [inside the function](#L292) rather than at the top of the file, which is unusual enough to need explaining. Task 2 ships as a **container**, a self-contained bundle of code and libraries that the organisers run on their own machines, and that container installs a hand-written list of packages. Scoring never happens inside it, so `surface-distance` is not on that list. A normal top-of-file import would run when the container starts and crash it. There is [an existing import in this file](#L389-L390) placed the same way for the same reason, so this follows a pattern that was already here.\n\n[Step 7](#step-7) is the argument this function takes that is easiest to get wrong."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 338,
      "node": "loo",
      "title": "The spacing argument, and why passing the obvious value would be wrong",
      "summary": "leave_one_out reads each subject's real voxel dimensions off the file. They are nothing like cubes, and NSD is measured in millimetres.",
      "description": "[`leave_one_out`](#L325-L353) is the loop that drives the whole protocol: 23 times, it fits the model on 22 patients and predicts the 23rd. It is the function that calls the scoring code, so it is where the new metric gets the one piece of information it cannot work out for itself.\n\nNSD is measured in millimetres, and a mask on its own is just an array of true and false. To convert a distance in array steps into a distance in millimetres you need the physical size of a voxel, which every MRI file carries in its header. [Line 338](#L338) reads it.\n\nHere is why that line is worth a step of its own. It would be very easy to pass `(1, 1, 1)`, and it would look right, because the model works internally on a 1mm grid. **These scans are not 1mm.** Their voxels are roughly 0.43 to 0.9 mm across within a slice, and the slices themselves are **5.2 to 6.75 mm** thick. They are not cubes; they are thin tiles. Handing the metric `(1, 1, 1)` would shrink every through-slice distance by about a factor of six and quietly inflate the score.\n\nQuietly is the operative word. The organisers' wrapper catches every exception and returns 0, so a shape or unit mistake there produces a plausible-looking number instead of a crash. This implementation deliberately does not catch anything, following the repository's standing instruction to fail loudly, and [a test](src/fomo_tune/test_main_task2.py#L75-L81) asserts that changing the spacing changes the answer, so a regression to `(1, 1, 1)` fails the suite rather than silently rewriting the results.\n\nHold on to those slice thicknesses. They are the explanation for the branch's main finding, which arrives in [step 19](#step-19).\n\nWith the spacing in hand, the scoring loop can run."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 301,
      "node": "curves",
      "title": "Scoring the same prediction at eighty different cutoffs",
      "summary": "subject_curves is where a probability map becomes numbers. It sweeps every candidate cutoff and records Dice and NSD at each.",
      "description": "Still in the protocol half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1). [`subject_curves`](#L301-L322) is called once per patient by [`leave_one_out`](#L325), the loop from [step 7](#step-7), and it is where a model's output turns into a score.\n\nThe model does not produce a mask. It produces a *probability* for every voxel, and somebody has to decide the cutoff above which a voxel counts as tumour. That choice matters a great deal, and rather than guess, the protocol tries every candidate in a fixed list and records what happens at each one. The result is a **curve** per patient per metric, which is where the function gets its name.\n\nThe loop body is short. [`binarize`](#L228-L237) applies one cutoff and returns a mask, [line 320](#L320) computes Dice from the overlap counts directly, and [line 321](#L321) hands the same mask to the metric from [step 6](#step-6). One new line, one new array, and the function's return grows from two things to three.\n\nNotice that Dice is computed inline in three arithmetic operations while NSD needs a library. That is the honest difference between the two: overlap is counting, and surface agreement is geometry.\n\nThis is also the branch's main cost. The loop now runs the geometry 80 times per patient per fold, which is 1,840 surface computations per run, and it is why a run went from about 170 seconds to 350. The library crops both masks to their shared bounding box internally before doing any work, which is what keeps that from being far worse.\n\nThe list of cutoffs it sweeps is the next step, and it is the one part of this commit that required permission."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 274,
      "node": "thr",
      "title": "The cutoff list had to reach past 0.5, and that is a frozen-file edit",
      "summary": "THRESHOLDS is protocol, so changing it invalidates recorded scores. It was changed anyway, in a shape that cannot invalidate anything.",
      "description": "Still in [`main_task2.py`](#L1), just below the [`Task2Method`](#L129) class and inside the protocol section, [`THRESHOLDS`](#L274) is the list [`subject_curves`](#L301) sweeps, and it is the single most sensitive line in the file, because it sits in the frozen half described in [step 3](#step-3).\n\nThe old list ran from a millionth up to [about 0.501](#D227). That range was not arbitrary. Tumour voxels are roughly 0.024 percent of a scan, so the old head's probabilities clustered down near that value and a *geometric* list, one that steps by multiplication rather than addition, put most of its candidates where the action was.\n\nA trained decoder does not behave that way. It is trained with losses that push its outputs toward 0 and 1, so its best cutoff can sit anywhere, including above 0.5. With the old list, such a model would be scored at a cutoff it never wanted, and the number would be wrong in a way nobody could see.\n\nSo the list was extended, and the shape of the extension is the whole argument. It is not a new evenly-spaced list from 0 to 1. It is the **old sixty values, unchanged, with twenty more added above them**, so the old list is contained inside the new one exactly. Any cutoff that was previously selectable still is, which means no score already written down can move. [A test](src/fomo_tune/test_main_task2.py#L37-L41) asserts that containment directly, and [step 17](#step-17) describes the run that confirmed it empirically.\n\nThe repository's `AGENTS.md` says editing the protocol \"makes the resulting score incomparable to every score already recorded, so it is a deliberate decision rather than a step in an experiment\". The superset shape is what makes this a cheap decision rather than an expensive one, and it is the thing to raise with the maintainer rather than let him find in a diff.\n\nIt also turned out to matter in practice rather than only in principle. One fold of the decoder run selected a cutoff of **0.857**, which the old list could not have offered."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 366,
      "node": "score",
      "title": "Two metrics out of one bootstrap",
      "summary": "score grows a loop over both metrics, and both resample the same patients so their intervals can be compared.",
      "description": "Back in the protocol half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1), in [`score`](#L356-L381), the reporting function from [step 4](#step-4).\n\nThe body became [a loop over two metrics](#L366-L378) rather than a single block, and each pass reports four things: the mean, a confidence interval, an oracle, and the cutoff that metric would pick. Two details in it are deliberate.\n\nFirst, the **resampling is drawn once, above the loop**, at [line 364](#L364). The confidence intervals here are computed by a *bootstrap*: pretend the 23 patients are the whole world, draw 23 of them at random with repeats, recompute the mean, do that 2,000 times, and read the spread. Drawing one set of resamples and using it for both metrics means the Dice interval and the NSD interval are built from the same 2,000 imaginary cohorts, so the two are directly comparable. Drawing separately would leave them differing partly by luck.\n\nSecond, the last line before the return, [line 380](#L380), keeps a key called `threshold` pointing at the Dice-optimal cutoff. That is what the shipped model uses. The NSD-optimal cutoff is reported alongside it under a different name, purely as information. This is what makes the metric commit inert: it adds numbers to the report and changes no behaviour at all, so the model that gets submitted is byte-identical to the one before it.\n\nThat completes the scoring half. The next six steps are the model half, starting with the thing that replaces the logistic regression."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 97,
      "node": "decoder",
      "title": "ConvDecoder, three doublings from tokens to voxels",
      "summary": "A small network that takes the 26x30x26 grid of tokens and grows it back to 208x240x208 values, one per voxel.",
      "description": "We now leave the protocol half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1) for the method half, the part [step 3](#step-3) described as the one you are meant to change. We have jumped back up the file to the [constructor](#L97-L108) where the layers are built. [`ConvDecoder`](#L93-L114) is the replacement for the logistic regression from [step 2](#step-2), and it is the reason the branch exists.\n\nThe idea is simple once the shapes are clear. The encoder hands over a grid of 26 x 30 x 26 positions, each holding 1024 numbers. The answer needs to be 208 x 240 x 208 positions each holding 1 number. That is eight times larger on every side, and eight is two doubled three times, so the network doubles three times.\n\n![Each row is one stage: the spatial grid grows eightfold while the channel count shrinks from 1024 to 1.](.youngblood/assets/task2-finetune/fig3-decoder.png)\n\nTwo pieces of machinery do all the work. A **3D convolution** slides a small window over the volume and computes a weighted sum at each position, so unlike the old logistic regression it can see a cube's neighbours rather than judging each in isolation. And `F.interpolate` at [line 111](#L111) is plain trilinear upsampling, which doubles the grid by smoothly filling in between existing values, adding no parameters at all.\n\nSo each round of the [loop at line 110](#L110-L113) doubles the grid, then convolves, then applies **GELU**, a smooth activation function that lets the network represent things a stack of plain sums could not. The [1 x 1 x 1 convolution](#L99) before the loop is not spatial at all; a window of one voxel just mixes the 1024 channels down to 32, which is what makes the rest affordable.\n\nThis architecture is not invented here. It is a reconstruction of one a colleague described in a chat message and did not publish, which is why it is being rebuilt rather than imported.\n\n[Step 12](#step-12) is the shape problem that has to be solved before this can be called at all."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 176,
      "node": "tokengrid",
      "title": "Putting the tokens back where they came from",
      "summary": "The encoder returns only the tokens it kept, as a flat list. A convolution needs them laid out in space.",
      "description": "Still in the method half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1), now inside the [`Task2Method`](#L129) class that holds the model. [`token_grid`](#L176-L181) is a small function solving a mismatch between what the encoder hands back and what a convolution needs.\n\nThe encoder does not return all 20,280 tokens. It skips cubes that contain no brain, and what comes out of [`embed`](#L149-L159) is a flat list of the ones it kept plus a list of their positions in the grid. A flat list is fine for a logistic regression, which looks at each token alone. It is useless to a convolution, which is defined entirely by which positions are next to which.\n\nSo this function reverses the flattening. [Line 179](#L179) allocates a grid of zeros with a slot for every position, [line 180](#L180) scatters the kept tokens into their slots, and [line 181](#L181) transposes and reshapes it into the layout PyTorch expects, channels before space.\n\nThe zeros are meaningful rather than a filler value. A position that stays zero is a cube the encoder judged to contain no brain, and the decoder gets to learn what a hole means. The old code had exactly the same convention, [written a few lines apart](#D163-D165), so this is a familiar idea in a new shape rather than a new one.\n\nThis is also the memory question answered. That grid is about 83 megabytes, and it is the largest thing the training loop touches, because the encoder itself is frozen and never needs gradients. That is why all of this fits on a laptop GPU, which [step 14](#step-14) returns to.\n\nBefore the training loop, the thing it optimises."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 117,
      "node": "loss",
      "title": "A loss that survives a tumour being 0.024 percent of the scan",
      "summary": "Two loss terms added together, because each one alone fails in a different way on data this unbalanced.",
      "description": "[`segmentation_loss`](#L117-L126) sits between [`ConvDecoder`](#L93) and the training loop, both in the method half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1). A **loss** is the single number training tries to make small: it measures how wrong a prediction is, and every adjustment to the network is chosen to reduce it.\n\nThe difficulty is the imbalance. Roughly one voxel in four thousand is tumour, and a network that answers \"not tumour\" everywhere is already right 99.976 percent of the time. Any loss that rewards being right on average will happily settle there.\n\nSo two terms are added. **Binary cross-entropy** at [line 121](#L121) scores each voxel independently, and its [`pos_weight` argument](#L121) multiplies the penalty for missing a tumour voxel, here by 100, which is what stops the all-empty answer from being comfortable. **Soft Dice** at [lines 123 to 125](#L123-L125) is the Dice score from [step 4](#step-4) written so it can be differentiated, using raw probabilities in place of a hard mask, and it scores the prediction as a whole shape rather than voxel by voxel.\n\nNeither works alone, which is what the [docstring](#L118-L119) records. Cross-entropy on its own is satisfied too easily. Soft Dice on its own gives a network that overlaps the tumour nowhere almost no signal to follow, because its numerator stays near zero and so does its gradient. Together, one pulls the prediction into the neighbourhood and the other shapes it once it arrives.\n\nThe `+ 1` on both halves of [line 125](#L125) is a smoothing constant that keeps the fraction defined when a prediction is empty. [Two tests](src/fomo_tune/test_main_task2.py#L129-L146) pin the behaviour that matters: a confident right answer scores better than a confident wrong one, and a partly-right answer scores better than predicting nothing.\n\nNow the loop that uses it."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 188,
      "node": "fit",
      "title": "A new decoder for every fold, and why that line is the dangerous one",
      "summary": "fit trains the decoder from scratch. The first line of its body is the one that would silently invalidate the entire experiment.",
      "description": "[`fit`](#L183-L214) is where the method half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1) changed the most. It used to be three lines that handed some arrays to a statistics library. It is now a training loop.\n\n**[Line 188](#L188) is the line to look at first.** It builds a brand new [`ConvDecoder`](#L93) every time `fit` is called, throwing away whatever was there before. That looks wasteful and it is the most important line in the function. Recall from [step 7](#step-7) that the protocol calls `fit` 23 times, once per fold, each time on a different 22 patients. If the decoder carried over between calls, fold 5's model would have already been trained on the patient fold 5 is about to be tested on. Every score would be inflated and nothing in the output would look wrong. The [docstring](#L184-L185) says so, because a reader who does not know the protocol calls this repeatedly would otherwise read the line as a mistake and tidy it away.\n\nThe rest is a small standard loop. [Line 190](#L190) is **AdamW**, the optimiser, the algorithm that decides how to adjust the weights given the loss. [Line 191](#L191) is a learning-rate schedule borrowed from the pretraining half of this repository, which starts small, rises, and then decays; borrowing it rather than writing one keeps this consistent with how the rest of the project trains. Each pass picks one patient at [line 203](#L203), builds its token grid, computes the loss, and takes a step.\n\nThe one non-obvious ingredient is [line 189](#L189), **EMA**, an exponential moving average of the weights. Alongside the network being trained it keeps a second copy that lags behind, following the training weights slowly. Late in training the weights bounce around a good region rather than sitting in it, and the average of that bouncing is usually better than any single point on the path. The `update_after_step` argument makes the shadow copy track exactly until step 100 and only start averaging after that. [Line 214](#L214) keeps the average and discards the original.\n\n[Step 15](#step-15) is where the trained decoder finally gets used."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 222,
      "node": "proba",
      "title": "The line that deletes the staircase",
      "summary": "predict_proba is the function both scoring and submission go through. Ten lines of broadcasting become one call.",
      "description": "Still in the method half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1), at the last of the functions [`Task2Method`](#L129) uses to make a prediction. [`predict_proba`](#L217-L226) is the most-run function in the file. [Step 3](#step-3) noted that the scored path and the submitted path both go through it, so this is simultaneously what the protocol measures and what the competition container will execute.\n\nIts job has not changed: take a patient's images, return a probability for every voxel, on that patient's own grid. What changed is the middle.\n\nThe old version ran the logistic regression to get one number per cube and then called [`repeat`](#D169-D178), whose whole purpose was to copy each of those numbers across all 512 voxels of its cube. That block was the staircase from [step 2](#step-2). Not a consequence of it, not a symptom of it. It was the thing itself.\n\nIt is now [two lines](#L221-L222): build the token grid from [step 12](#step-12), run the decoder from [step 11](#step-11), and squash the output through a sigmoid to turn raw scores into probabilities between 0 and 1. The `[0, 0]` strips the batch and channel dimensions that PyTorch requires and this code has no use for.\n\n[Line 225](#L225) is unchanged and worth noticing anyway. The decoder works on a standard 208 x 240 x 208 millimetre grid, and every patient's scan has its own size, position and voxel shape. That line resamples the prediction back onto the patient's own grid, because the mask the challenge scores has to line up with the scan it came from.\n\nThe decorator on [line 216](#L216) is new: it tells PyTorch not to track gradients here, since nothing in this function is being trained. Saving and loading also changed, from a statistics-library format to [a PyTorch one](#L246-L262), because what gets stored is now network weights rather than regression coefficients.\n\nThat is the last of the code. [Step 16](#step-16) puts the whole route back together before the results start."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py",
      "line": 325,
      "node": "loo",
      "title": "The whole route, in one pass",
      "summary": "A restatement of the fifteen steps so far, following one patient from scan to score.",
      "description": "The walk returns to the protocol half of [`main_task2.py`](#L1) one last time. Standing on [`leave_one_out`](#L325-L353), the loop that drives everything, here is the entire path in one piece now that all the parts exist.\n\nA patient's FLAIR scan goes into the frozen encoder, which dices it into 8mm cubes and returns one 1024-number token for each cube that contains brain. [`token_grid`](#L176) scatters those tokens back into their spatial positions, filling the skipped cubes with zeros. [`ConvDecoder`](#L93) takes that 26 x 30 x 26 grid and doubles it three times, convolving at each stage, until it is 208 x 240 x 208 with a single number per voxel. A sigmoid in [`predict_proba`](#L217) makes those numbers probabilities, and they are resampled onto the patient's own grid.\n\nThe protocol takes it from there. [`subject_curves`](#L301) sweeps all 80 cutoffs, computing Dice by counting overlap and NSD through the DeepMind library, using the patient's real voxel dimensions. That happens 23 times, once per held-out patient, with [`fit`](#L183) training a fresh decoder on the other 22 each time. [`score`](#L356) reduces the 23 curves to a mean and a bootstrap interval per metric.\n\nTwo things about that route are worth carrying into the results. The decoder can now express a boundary anywhere, not only on cube edges, which is what the change was for. And the metric that measures boundaries is being computed for the first time, on grids whose slices are six millimetres thick.\n\nThe explanation ends here. Everything from [step 17](#step-17) on is judgment: what was run, what came out, and one result that argues against the reason the change was made."
    },
    {
      "file": "experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md",
      "line": 35,
      "node": "ran",
      "title": "A control run, because the baseline did not reproduce",
      "summary": "The rerun scored 0.181 where the team's table says 0.195. That gap had to be explained before any result could be trusted.",
      "description": "Every run lives in [`experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv`](experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md#L1), a directory holding the commands, the logs and the numbers, following a convention this repository already uses for its other experiments.\n\nThree runs were done: the old logistic head with the new metric, and the conv decoder twice with different random seeds. All three use the same pretrained model, the repository's default.\n\nThe first result was a problem. The old head scored Dice **0.181**, and the team's own leaderboard records **0.195** for what should be the same thing. A changed protocol is the obvious suspect, and if the change had moved the score then every comparison afterwards would be meaningless.\n\nSo before anything else, a control: the unmodified file from before the branch, old cutoff list, no NSD, run on this machine. It produced `0.1805574242368666`, and an oracle and a chosen cutoff that also matched, **every digit identical** to the rerun. The metric commit moves Dice by exactly zero, which is what [step 10](#step-10) predicted from reading the code and is now measured rather than argued.\n\nThe real cause is elsewhere. 21 of the 23 folds match the recorded log to three decimals and two flip hard: one patient falls from 0.494 to 0.022 and another rises from 0.400 to 0.530. There is a post-processing step that keeps only the largest connected blob of predicted voxels, and it is discontinuous, so a hair of difference in the statistics library changes which blob wins. The original was run on a datacentre GPU with an older library; this was a laptop. One patient flipping moves a 23-patient mean by about 0.02, which is the whole gap.\n\nThe consequence is a rule for reading everything below: **the decoder is compared against 0.181 measured here, never against 0.195 measured elsewhere.** That is why the old head was rerun at all rather than taking the recorded number.\n\n[Step 18](#step-18) is what the comparison shows."
    },
    {
      "file": "experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md",
      "line": 60,
      "node": "dice",
      "title": "On Dice the decoder clearly works",
      "summary": "Two seeds, both about +0.08 paired against the old head, both intervals clear of zero and both far larger than the seed noise.",
      "description": "The results section of the experiment's [README](experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md#L60) holds the tables. On Dice the answer is unambiguous.\n\nThe old head scores 0.181 and the decoder scores **0.258** and **0.266** on two seeds. Compared *paired*, meaning patient by patient rather than mean against mean, the difference is **+0.078** and **+0.085**, and neither 95 percent interval touches zero.\n\nTwo seeds were run rather than one on purpose. The repository's `AGENTS.md` warns against reporting a win you cannot separate from run-to-run noise, and the colleague who first built this architecture saw his own two seeds differ by 0.033. Here the two seeds differ by **0.007**, so the effect is more than ten times the noise around it. Without that second run there would be a single number and no way to tell.\n\nThe two values also sit inside the range he reported for the same architecture, 0.228 through 0.313, which is about as much agreement as a reconstruction from a chat message can honestly claim.\n\nOne caution the numbers do not show. The improvement is not spread evenly across patients: it is a handful of large wins, with one patient going from 0.000 to 0.633 and another from 0.140 to 0.687, and one patient regressing from 0.253 to 0.000. A mean over 23 subjects with that shape is a fragile thing, and [step 20](#step-20) comes back to it.\n\nThe metric this change was supposedly *for* is the next step."
    },
    {
      "file": "experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md",
      "line": 81,
      "node": "surf",
      "title": "On NSD it does not, and the scans explain why",
      "summary": "Both NSD intervals cross zero. The reason is the slice thickness, which no change to the head can touch.",
      "description": "Still in the experiment's [README](experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md#L1), one section below the tables from [step 18](#step-18). This is the branch's actual finding, and it contradicts the argument that motivated the branch.\n\nThe reasoning going in was that a staircase boundary must be hurting a boundary metric, so a head that can draw smoothly should help NSD most of all. Measured, it does not. NSD moves **+0.024** and **+0.043** across the two seeds, and **both intervals include zero** while both Dice intervals exclude it.\n\n![Paired differences against the same-machine old head. Both Dice bars sit right of the line; both NSD bars cross it.](.youngblood/assets/task2-finetune/fig5-results.png)\n\nThe explanation is the number from [step 7](#step-7). NSD asks whether the boundary is within **1 mm** of the truth, and these scans have slices **5.2 to 6.75 mm** thick. Within a slice, voxel edges are well under a millimetre apart, so a boundary can always land inside the tolerance. Across slices, the nearest place a boundary can possibly go is a whole slice away, several times the tolerance. Most of the through-plane surface cannot score, and no head can change that, because it is a property of how the scan was acquired.\n\n![Each tick is a place a boundary can sit. In plane, several fall inside the 1mm band. Through plane, none do.](.youngblood/assets/task2-finetune/fig4-tolerance.png)\n\nA second measurement supports this. If you take the true masks and ask what the best possible 8mm-block answer would score, you get about 0.449 on an isotropic 1mm grid, and the old head only reached 0.172 when scored the way the challenge scores it. It was nowhere near the block ceiling, so the blocks were never what was limiting NSD.\n\nThe clearest evidence is two patients where the metrics move in opposite directions. One goes from Dice 0.611 to 0.579 while its NSD collapses from 0.427 to 0.119; another improves on Dice while its NSD falls from 0.243 to 0.031. Better volume, worse boundary. A Dice-only protocol cannot see that at all, which is the argument for the first commit made better than any reasoning could."
    },
    {
      "file": "experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md",
      "line": 101,
      "node": "left",
      "title": "Twelve of twenty-three patients still score zero",
      "summary": "The headline mean hides a bimodal result. Half the cohort fails completely under both heads.",
      "description": "The last section of the experiment [README](experiments/fomo_tune_task2_conv/README.md#L101) records what did not improve, which on this branch is most of it.\n\n**Twelve of the 23 patients score 0.000 Dice under both decoder seeds.** Not low, zero: the model finds nothing at all. The same patients scored zero under the old head. Those are the small tumours, and the mean of 0.258 is really eleven patients doing reasonably and twelve contributing nothing.\n\nThat reframes what the branch achieved. It made the model better at outlining tumours it can already find. It did nothing about finding them, and that is where most of the remaining score is.\n\nThree other limits belong on the record. Both decoder runs are single models with no ensembling, and the colleague's higher numbers of 0.302 and 0.313 came from combining models, so neither is reproduced here. Every number is from a laptop GPU, and [step 17](#step-17) showed that hardware alone moves this benchmark by 0.014. And with 23 patients, one subject flipping is worth about 0.02 on any mean here, which is a useful thing to hold next to any difference smaller than that.\n\nThere is also a loose end in the shipped model. The chosen cutoff still maximises Dice, as [step 10](#step-10) described, even though the ranking weighs NSD equally. Now that both curves are recorded in the saved output, choosing differently is a small experiment somebody could run without training anything.\n\n[Step 21](#step-21) closes the tour."
    },
    {
      "title": "What to check yourself on",
      "node": "close",
      "description": "The tour is over. If the walk worked, you can explain this branch to somebody else with the editor shut. These questions are the test, and predicting before you open each answer is worth more than reading them.\n\n<details><summary>Why could the old head only draw 8mm blocks, and which line was responsible?</summary>\n\nThe encoder summarises each 8mm cube as one token, and the old head produced one probability per token. To get a mask, [the code that ran next](src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py#D169-D178) copied that probability across all 512 voxels of its cube, so after the cutoff every cube was entirely in or entirely out. The encoder's cubes are fixed by the pretrained weights; the copying was a choice, and deleting it is most of [step 15](#step-15).\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>The threshold list is in a frozen part of the file. Why was extending it acceptable anyway?</summary>\n\nBecause the new list contains the old one exactly rather than replacing it. Every cutoff that could previously be selected still can, so no recorded score can move, and [a test](src/fomo_tune/test_main_task2.py#L37-L41) enforces that. A freshly-spaced list covering the same range would have looked equally reasonable and would have quietly changed old results.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>What would have happened if fit had not built a new decoder every call?</summary>\n\nThe protocol calls [`fit`](src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py#L183) once per fold, each time on 22 of 23 patients. A decoder carried between calls would arrive at fold 5 already trained on the patient fold 5 is about to be scored on. Every number would be inflated and nothing in the output would look wrong. That is why [line 188](src/fomo_tune/main_task2.py#L188) exists and why its docstring explains itself.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Why did the rerun of the old head score 0.181 when the team's table says 0.195?</summary>\n\nDifferent hardware and a different version of the statistics library. The control run proved the branch was innocent: unmodified pre-change code on this machine gives the identical `0.1805574242368666`. The mechanism is the largest-blob filter, which is discontinuous, so a tiny numerical difference flips which blob wins on two patients, and one flipped patient is worth about 0.02 on a 23-patient mean.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>The decoder can draw a smooth boundary now. Why did that not improve the boundary metric?</summary>\n\nNSD asks for the boundary within 1 mm, and these scans have 5 to 7 mm slices, so across slices the nearest place a boundary can sit is several times the tolerance away. That limit comes from the acquisition, not from the head. The supporting evidence is that the old head scored 0.172 against a block-level ceiling of roughly 0.449, so it was never near the limit the blocks imposed.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>What is the largest remaining problem, and is it the one this branch was about?</summary>\n\nTwelve of 23 patients score exactly zero under every version tried, old and new. Those are the small tumours, and the failure is detection rather than outlining. The branch improved outlining. No, it is not the problem the branch was about, which is the most useful thing to know before choosing what to do next.\n</details>\n\nIf any of those did not come out cleanly, that is a defect in the tour rather than in you. Running `/youngblood learn` puts an agent beside these steps that knows which one you are on, answers the question, and edits the step so the next reader does not have to ask."
    }
  ]
}